<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kosovo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1389">1389</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> was fought on 15 June 1389 on the Kosovo field near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a> between a Serbian-led coalition under Prince Lazar and the Ottoman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>. Both rulers were killed, and although the outcome was not immediately decisive, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> soon became an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The battle became a central theme of Serbian epic poetry and national memory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo.jpg" alt="Battle of Kosovo" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Stefanović · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Kosovo,_Adam_Stefanovi%C4%87,_1870.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> in December 1371 and the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a> earlier that year, the Serbian Empire had broken into virtually independent principalities. Prince Lazar, ruler of Moravian Serbia, the northern part of the former empire, prepared for a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. After Ottoman defeats at Pločnik in 1386 and Bileća in 1388, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> moved his troops through Kratovo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumanovo">Kumanovo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a>, where he arrived on 14 June 1389. Lazar gathered troops near Niš and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, a crossroads that let him control the routes Murad could take. Lazar's army included his own troops, a contingent under his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> and Bosnian forces under Vlatko Vuković sent by King Tvrtko I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Reliable accounts are scarce, and no first-hand account from a participant exists. The army sizes are disputed; the leading estimate accepted by Western scholars is 15,000 to 20,000 for Lazar and 27,000 to 30,000 for Murad. Murad's son Bayezid commanded the right wing and his son Yakub the left. It is believed that the battle began with Ottoman archers shooting at the Serbian cavalry, which broke through the Ottoman left wing, but light Ottoman cavalry and infantry then counterattacked and the Serbian heavy armour became a disadvantage. Bayezid led a counterattack and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> routed the Serbian infantry.</p><p>Lazar was captured and Murad was killed. Murad's death is attributed to a Serbian knight named Miloš Obilić, and accounts of how he did it differ. In popular tradition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> betrayed Lazar by fleeing, a theory first presented by Mavro Orbini in 1601 that is largely seen as unfounded. Both armies were largely destroyed, and the battle is the only time an Ottoman sultan was killed in battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Bayezid killed his brother Yakub on hearing of their father's death and became sole heir. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> had too few men left to field large armies, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> could draw on reserves from the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> tried to expand in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and the Ottomans renewed their campaign in the south in 1390 to 1391. Some Serbian principalities that were not already <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> became so in the following years, and Lazar's daughter married Bayezid. Serbia became an Ottoman province, and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> on 20 June 1459 marks the end of medieval Serbian statehood.</p><p>The battle was mythologised soon afterwards, and the Kosovo Myth became central to Serbian folklore. In the 19th century Vuk Karadžić collected the epic poems of the Kosovo cycle. The battle is seen in Serbian public discourse as important to Serbian history, tradition and national identity, and Vidovdan commemorates it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kosovo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moravian Serbia under Lazar Hrebeljanović became the strongest successor and fought the Ottomans at the <b>Battle of Kosovo</b> in 1389.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cominform chose 28 June 1948 to expel Yugoslavia: the anniversary of the <b>Battle of Kosovo</b> Field in 1389, the assassination of Archduke Ferdinand at Sarajevo in 1914 and the Vidovdan Constitution of 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Lazar died at the <b>Battle of Kosovo</b> in 1389.</blockquote><a href="/state/moravian-serbia">Moravian Serbia</a></li><li><blockquote>They joined coalitions against the Balšići and against the Ottomans; Theodore II Muzaka died fighting at <b>Kosovo</b> in 1389 according to tradition.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-berat">Principality of Berat</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>battle of Kosovo</b> in 1389, Lazar's son Stefan Lazarević ruled Moravian Serbia as an Ottoman vassal.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-despotate">Serbian Despotate</a></li></ul></section>
